  "response": "Claims of quantum advantage are adjudicated as contests between two moving targets: the measured performance of a device, and the best classical procedure anyone has yet devised for the same task. The dispute over the first large sampling experiment illustrates the procedure. Google announced a quantum supremacy result using its Sycamore processor in October 2019, and the content of that announcement confirms that the processor used 53 superconducting qubits for the random circuit sampling task. Google reported that the random circuit sampling task took about 200 seconds on Sycamore, whereas it estimated that the same sampling task would require about 10,000 years on the Summit supercomputer.\n\nThe decisive move in such disputes is never an inspection of the quantum processor; it is an attack on the classical estimate, and Google's October announcement drew one promptly. IBM responded that the Sycamore task could be completed classically in about 2.5 days using secondary storage on Summit. IBM's rebuttal stated that Summit could finish the Sycamore sampling task in 11 minutes. A classical completion measured in days cannot be squared with a projection of ten thousand years: the two cannot both be correct accounts of the cost of the same workload. After that exchange, Pan, Chen and Zhang reported a tensor-network classical simulation of the Sycamore sampling task on a GPU cluster. That simulation demonstrates what a later benchmarking review concluded, namely that the 10,000-year classical runtime estimate no longer held for the Sycamore task.\n\nA second line of adjudication asks whether the statistic used to certify the experiment can itself be reproduced without a quantum device. Linear cross-entropy benchmarking is the fidelity measure used to verify random circuit sampling experiments; classical spoofing algorithms, by contrast, have achieved cross-entropy benchmarking scores comparable to those of the quantum hardware.\n\nThe same pattern of retraction appears in algorithmic theory, where advantage claims rest on asymptotic complexity comparisons rather than on wall-clock timings. Ewin Tang published a classical algorithm for the recommendation systems problem in 2018, and that algorithm caused the exponential advantage of the quantum recommendation system over classical methods to disappear. Both the spoofing results and the collapse of that exponential separation show that credible advantage claims require benchmarking the quantum runtime against the best known classical algorithm rather than a naive simulation method. Researchers therefore treat any reported separation as provisional: it stands only until a classical method narrows it, and the burden lies with the claimant to specify the classical baseline precisely enough that others can attempt to beat it.",
